Datasets. Following [8,10], we employ the following three kinds of benchmarks to comprehensively evaluate the model's capability in long-term complex tool usage: (1) Mathematical Reasoning, including the typical math problems like GSM8K [6] and MATH [21]; and competitive math problem like AIME24 [96], AIME25 [97] and MATH500 [43];

(2) Knowledge-Intensive Reasoning, including four multi-hop question-answering problems based on Wikipedia, i.e., HotpotQA [89], 2WikiMultihopQA [22], Musique [73] and Bamboogle [54]. We also include the web multi-hop task WebWalker [81];

(3) Deep Search, including the challenging General AI Assistant (GAIA) [51], Humanity's Last Exam [53], and information retrieval tasks like WebWalker [81] and Xbench [4].

Baselines. Four kinds of baselines are selected for comprehensive comparison: (1) Vanilla RL Method, including GRPO [18], Reinforce++ [24], DAPO [92], GPPO [68] and CISPO [3]; (2) Agentic RL Method, including GIGPO [14] and ARPO [10];

(3) LLM Backbones, where we adopt the instruct version of Llama3.1-8B [11] and Qwen2.5 [26] as the generalized backbones; we also report the results of QwQ [72], DeepSeek-R1 [18], GPT-4o [27], o1-preview [27] and Qwen3-32B [88];

(4) Search Agents and others, where we adopt the Search-o1 [37] and Webthinker [39]; For other mechanisms we employ the RAG [35] and ReAct [91] framework. Metrics. We employ the F1-score to evaluate the performance of models over four question-answering tasks. For other tasks, we employ LLM-as-a-Judge and deploy a Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct model with vLLM for correctness calculation. We use pass@1 over the LLM-judged results.

Implementation Details. Following [47], we implement the decay rate in procedure advantage as γ = 2 -1 τ and keeps τ = 32. The overall methodology is implemented on the VeRL framework [63] where tool-call results are detached from gradient computation. The batch-size is set to 128 and a PPO mini-batchsize of 16, resulting in 8 gradient updates in one step. We also set the coefficient β = 0 to stabilize training. We train APPO in both reasoning and search tasks following ARPO [10], where models are separately trained for 2 and 5 epochs. The search results are the top-10 snippets retrieved by Bing search engine following [31,40,9] and python code is executed in a sandbox environment. To ensure the model possesses basic tool-use capabilities, we directly adopt the SFT pipeline from ARPO [10]. More implementation details are attached to Appendix C.
